 			<p>“I hope the Vaughns are not fine, grown-up people. Do you know anything about them, Jo?” asked Meg.</p>
 			<p>“Only that there are four of them. Kate is older than you, Fred and Frank (twins) about my age, and a little girl (Grace), who is nine or ten. Laurie knew them abroad, and liked the boys; I fancied, from the way he primmed up his mouth in speaking of her, that he didn’t admire Kate much.”</p>
 			<p>“I’m so glad my French print is clean; it’s just the thing, and so becoming!” observed Meg complacently. “Have you anything decent, Jo?”</p>
-			<p>“Scarlet and gray boating suit, good enough for me. I shall row and tramp about, so I don’t want any starch to think of. You’ll come, Bethy?”</p>
+			<p>“Scarlet and gray boating suit, good enough for me. I shall row and tramp about, so I don’t want any starch to think of. You’ll come, <span epub:type="z3998:personal-name">Bethy</span>?”</p>
 			<p>“If you won’t let any of the boys talk to me.”</p>
 			<p>“Not a boy!”</p>
 			<p>“I like to please Laurie; and I’m not afraid of <abbr epub:type="z3998:name-title">Mr.</abbr> Brooke, he is so kind; but I don’t want to play, or sing, or say anything. I’ll work hard, and not trouble anyone; and you’ll take care of me, Jo, so I’ll go.”</p>
